>take form
Taken.

>fill form
You fill in the form at the counter, using the chained pen: Name, Arthur Dent.
Address, the sandwich hut, Lamuella. Purpose of visit: personal. There is a box
for Other, and you leave it blank, which seems to be correct.

Ford leans on the counter and clears his throat. "Have you ever considered," he
says to Gruba, "the mathematics of queues?"

[The score has just gone up by four points.]

>wait
Time passes.

"Queues," says Gruba, in the tone of one who has been lured by his favourite
subject and knows it, "are not a mathematical object." "Aren't they?" says Ford,
delighted.

>wait
Time passes.

"And another thing," says Ford loudly, and points out of the window. Gruba
turns, with something like longing, to look at the queue that is not there. His
stamp stands unattended.

[The score has just gone up by six points.]

>stamp form
While Gruba glares out of the window at the queue that isn't there, you reach
round the end of the glass, find the stamp by feel, and bring it down on the
form with the quiet efficiency of a man who has been to a great many offices.
The stamp has a small, smug crest.

[The score has just gone up by six points.]

>e
Gruba looks at the stamped form for slightly longer than necessary. "Next," he
says, in the voice of a man who has decided to let it go.

The Concourse
A wide, bright concourse under a roof like a very clean sky. Benches face
nothing in particular; shop windows display the sort of goods that make you
realise you've been away. A vending machine hums against one wall. Customs lies
back to the west, a sign points east to the Departure Gate, and a small doorway
to the south says LOST PROPERTY in letters that have given up.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

"This is a public announcement," says a very tired voice from the ceiling. "I'm
told it is a pleasure to serve you. I wouldn't know."

>s

Lost Property
A cramped office stacked to the ceiling with lost things: umbrellas, a trumpet,
one enormous boot, several thousand single gloves. The Attendant's counter is at
the back. The concourse is back to the north.

The Attendant stands behind the counter, with the air of a man who can find
anything and would prefer you hadn't lost it. 

You can also see an unclaimed sandwich here.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>ask attendant about norway
The Attendant's eyes close for a moment. "Brown, frayed, a stain shaped like the
coast of Norway," he says. "Slartibartfast's." He disappears among the shelves
and returns with your old towel, as though he had known all along. "Thanks for
describing it," he says. "They mostly can't."

[The score has just gone up by eight points.]

>take sandwich
The Attendant waves a hand without looking: "Take it. It has been there since
before the Collapse of Something." You take it. It is, as far as you can tell, a
honey sandwich.

